  <!-- Boomrose Arrow is the one vanilla arrow that carries a blast, and a wild or AI-placed turret
       is stocked by the game with nobody choosing the ammunition.
       MagazineAmmoLoader.GetAmmoBlueprints is opt-out: every blueprint with the ammo part is a
       candidate unless tagged, and an untagged one gets a single entry in the pool.

       It is not a chance of getting a bad arrow - it is a guaranteed share of them. Short Bow and
       Compound Bow both carry MagazineAmmoLoader MaxAmmo="1" AmmoPart="AmmoArrow";
       GetDesiredAmmoCount asks for AmmoPerAction * 50 = 50 arrows, and the fill is
       (desired / entries) of EVERY entry rather than a weighted pick. At 25 entries that was 2
       apiece, so a wild bow turret carried twelve effect arrows and one boomrose, every time.
       At 18 it is 18 steel, 6 carbide, 4 folded carbide and 2 of each end-game arrow.

       The Turbow is not affected either way: it uses EnergyAmmoLoader, so a Turbow turret stocks
       no arrows at all.

       Only the blast case is merged. The high-value arrows vanilla also leaves untagged - fullerite,
       crysteel, flawless crysteel, zetachrome - are left exactly as they are. They are better
       sticks, not hazards, and where Freehold draws its value ceiling is their economy to set. -->

  <object Name="Boomrose Arrow" Load="Merge">
    <tag Name="ExcludeFromTurretStock" />
  </object>

  <!-- Effect shells, revived in #145.

       These were dead on arrival, and not because of anything Mura wrote. A shotgun never asks its
       ammunition what to fire. MagazineAmmoLoader.HandleEvent(LoadAmmoEvent) reads the WEAPON:

           if (ProjectileObject.IsNullOrEmpty())
               E.Projectile = GetProjectileObjectEvent.GetFor(E.LoadedAmmo, ParentObject);
           else
               E.Projectile = GameObject.Create(ProjectileObject, ...);

       and all four weapons that take a shell hardcode one - ProjectileShotgunPellet on the Pump
       Shotgun, ProjectileCombatShotgunPellet on the Combat Shotgun, and our own two. So a shell's
       AmmoShotgunShell ProjectileObject was thrown away and the payload never existed. That is
       exactly the symptom recorded in #14: the ammo "degraded to plain ammo".

       The fix is vanilla's own, and it is the only three uses of an empty ProjectileObject in the
       game: the Grenade Launcher and the Dart Gun blank it to say "fire whatever is loaded". We
       merge the same blank onto both vanilla shotguns and set it on ours, in RangedWeapons.xml.
       Plain Shotgun Shell is unaffected - ProjectileShotgunShell and the two pellet blueprints are
       identical, BasePenetration 4 over 1d2, all inheriting BaseShotgunProjectile.

       THE PAYLOAD IS PER PELLET, and that governs every number below. MissileWeapon.Fire raises
       LoadAmmoEvent AmmoPerAction times and then DeepCopies the result up to ShotsPerAction, so one
       shell becomes eight projectiles (twenty-four from four shells in the Drum Shotgun), each with
       its own spread roll and its own copy of the payload part. Eight deliveries, not one.

       That is why only four of Mura's ten survive, and the mechanism did the sorting.

       AREA PAYLOADS CANNOT BE SCALED DOWN, because eight pellets multiply the geometry rather than
       the magnitude. GasGrenade.DoDetonate fills the impact cell AND all eight adjacent cells at
       the full Density, and Density is per cell - so dividing it thins the gas without shrinking
       anything. Eight pellets across a WeaponAccuracy 45 cone reach up to 72 gas cells from one
       shell, which no vanilla grenade at any tier approaches. FlashbangGrenade bottoms out at
       Radius 1 / Duration 1 per pellet. HEGrenade is worst: Explode deals Force/250 and propagates
       only while (Force - dealt) / 8 > 100, so Boomrose's 1,200 divided by eight is 150 - zero
       damage and no propagation at all, an explosion in name only. Worse, IGrenade detonates on the
       projectile's DEATH (BeforeDeathRemovalEvent, when Primed), so a pellet that hits nothing still
       goes off where it lands. Eight detonations regardless of aim. Cut: explosive, flash, poison
       gas, sleep gas.

       BLEEDING IS NOT AVAILABLE TO A PROJECTILE AT ALL. BleedingOnHit registers "WeaponHit" and
       "WieldedWeaponHit", and WeaponHit is raised in Combat.cs on the melee path - MissileWeapon
       never raises it, it raises "ProjectileHit". The razor shell would have fired and done nothing.
       So would the quill arrow we already shipped; that is #201. Cut: razor.

       Vibro, sunder and stasis are cut for the reasons #144 cut their arrows, each worse here
       because it would ride eight pellets from a tier 3 gun. Vorpal exists on three objects in the
       game and the nearest is a tier 7 heavy weapon. BasePenetration 10 is above every vanilla
       firearm projectile including the sniper rifle's 7. The stasis shell omitted
       IsRealityDistortionBased, which all three vanilla stasis grenades set, so normality would not
       have suppressed it.

       What survives is what a shotgun already is - many small hits - so the payload rides the hit.

       Heat and cold do NOT simply divide the arrow budget by eight, and the first attempt did,
       which is worth writing down because it shipped a payload that measurably did nothing.

       +30 and -6 a pellet look like parity: the arrows are +250 and -50 per action, a shell also
       spends one piece of ammunition per action, and eight pellets at -6 is -48. Play-testing found
       eight cryo shells into a 500 HP target that never got cold. The mechanism was fine - a probe
       build confirmed the shell's own projectile fires and TemperatureOnHit applies from it - and
       the number was the defect.

       Ambient regression is a FLOOR, not a curve. Temperature returns by Math.Max(5, |diff| * 0.02)
       every turn, a flat 5 at these magnitudes, and a shotgun connects with only a fraction of its
       pellets. So the ÷8 lands on top of a second division nobody chose, and what is left is
       measured against a fixed 5 a turn:

           -6 a pellet, 3 of 8 connect:  -18 a shot, and a turn spent moving hands +5 straight back
           -6 a pellet, 1 connects:      -6, which nets -1. The effect cannot accumulate.

       The arrow never had this problem because -50 against -5 is decisive on any single hit.

       The divisor is not eight either, and that is the second half of the same mistake. Play-testing
       -12 at point blank, standing still, took FIVE shots to freeze - about -150 delivered over five
       turns of regression, so roughly -30 a shot, so only about 2.5 of the eight pellets connect.
       MissileWeapon rolls Stat.Random(-WeaponAccuracy, WeaponAccuracy) per pellet at accuracy 45 on
       the Pump Shotgun, and that scatters most of them whatever the range.

       So the budget divides by the pellets that LAND, not the pellets fired. Two play-tests put that
       at about two - five shots at -12, then four at -20 - so -50 and +250 over 2 give -25 and +125
       a pellet. Three shots to freeze and two to ignite, which are the arrows' own figures reached
       the way a shotgun reaches them.

       Freeze DURATION is a second thing the per-pellet value sets, and it is easy to miss. What
       matters is how far past -100 the last shot lands, because regression claws back 5 a turn: at
       -20 a pellet the target tipped over to about -102 and thawed in one turn. At -25 a shot
       delivers about -50, so the run is 25 -> -20 -> -65 -> -110 and it stays down two to three
       turns, which is where the arrow sits. Tune the overshoot, not just the crossing.

       That still left DURATION unbounded, and play-testing found it: two shots at true point blank
       connected with about 4.4 pellets each, landed the target near -185, and held it frozen for
       roughly 17 turns. Duration is pure overshoot at 5 a turn, so it scales with however many
       pellets happen to land - and the measured rate runs anywhere from 1.8 to 4.4. No single Amount
       fixes that, because shaving it only moves which end of the variance is wrong.

       Max and MaxTemp bound it. TemperatureOnHit applies only while the target is still short of
       MaxTemp, on whichever side the Amount points:

           (Amount.RollMaxCached() >= 0) ? (Temperature < MaxTemp) : (Temperature > MaxTemp)

       Vanilla uses this for heat on four objects, always Max="true" MaxTemp="400", so the incendiary
       shell takes that verbatim. The cold side has no vanilla precedent but the same branch supports
       it, and the value has one trap: MaxTemp MUST sit below the -100 brittle line. At -90 the cold
       would stop applying around -95, above the line, and the target would never freeze at all - the
       cap would silently defeat the payload. At -110 the last applicable pellet lands between -110
       and -135, so the freeze is guaranteed and it holds two to seven turns rather than seventeen.

       A perfect eight-pellet hit is still the fastest freeze; it is the duration that no longer runs
       away with it.

       The general form, because #146 will meet it with a chaingun dividing by six: a per-action
       budget cannot be rescaled to per-hit without checking the result against whatever decays per
       turn. Division eventually falls under a fixed floor, and below it the part fires, the event
       reaches the target, and nothing observable happens.

       One thing worth knowing about both: ProjectileHit fires even when a pellet FAILS to penetrate
       (the Penetrations = 0 branch raises it too) and TemperatureOnHit never checks penetration.
       Armour stops the damage, not the heat or the cold. It is also why the cryo shell is the
       family's answer to everything the takedown shell cannot touch: Physics.IsFrozen is nothing but
       Temperature <![CDATA[<=]]> -100 and Frozen.Apply has no conditions whatsoever, so an ooze, a
       slug or a worm freezes exactly as well as a humanoid.

       The flechette shell is new, and it is here because a real slug shell is not expressible. Shot
       count belongs to the gun - num9 is read straight off MissileWeapon.ShotsPerAction with no
       event in between, and GetMissileWeaponPerformanceEvent, the one event ammunition can reach,
       carries penetration and damage but no count. That is filed upstream as #200. So the
       anti-armour round reaches its role through penetration instead: eight steel darts rather than
       one slug. Pen 6 over flat 1 damage was picked by simulating Stat.RollDamagePenetrations
       directly - it lands within 10% of standard shot everywhere below AV 10 and 48% ahead at AV 12,
       which is the most populous declared AV in the game. Pen 7 and 1d2 damage were both tried and
       both beat standard shot nearly everywhere, which makes them upgrades rather than trades.

       The takedown shell carries two parts because they partition cleanly and neither can stack:
       Prone.Apply opens with a HasEffect<Prone> guard, and GroundOnHit extends a Grounded duration
       rather than adding a second one. So eight pellets buy reliability, never magnitude - which is
       what makes Chance an honest dial here and is exactly what StickyOnHit could not offer. That
       part reaches every anatomy and grounds fliers by itself, but it has no Chance field, Stuck has
       no anti-stack guard, and GameObject.ApplyEffect does not deduplicate, so one shell would apply
       eight separate Stuck effects and escaping rolls a save against each. No dial, so no shell.

       Chance 40 a pellet is about a 64% takedown per shell with the two pellets that typically land,
       degrading with distance as fewer connect. It was 12, sized against all eight pellets, which is
       the same arithmetic error the temperature values made: at 12 a shell had a 22% chance of doing
       anything at all before the save was even rolled, and play-testing saw nothing fire.

       The two SaveTargets are deliberately different, and copying one to the other was the second
       defect here. ProneOnHit rolls against 25 flat. GroundOnHit subtracts the target's flying level
       first, because FlyingLevelAidsSave is exactly what its name says:

           num2 = SaveTarget; if (FlyingLevelAidsSave) num2 -= flyingLevel;
           if (target.MakeSave(SaveStat, num2, ...)) return;   // passed, no grounding

       So 25 against a Gamma Moth, which carries Wings at level 10, is a save of 15 - trivial for a
       level 39 creature, and six point-blank shells failed to ground it. Vanilla's only user of the
       part writes SaveTarget="40" for precisely this reason, and that is what it takes here too: a
       strong flier still resists, which is the intent, but an ordinary one does not walk it.

       Worth knowing what Prone is and is not, because the name promises more than Qud delivers:
       -6 Agility, -5 DV, -80 move speed, and a turn spent standing up, for Duration 1. It does NOT
       stop the target attacking, so a prone creature next to you still bites. Prone.Apply also opens
       with a HasEffect<Prone> guard, so it neither stacks nor refreshes - a second shell into a
       target already down is wasted. The shell buys a window and an action, not an incapacitation.

       And the anatomy exclusion is wider than "creatures without legs". LimbSupportsProneness wants a
       Feet or Roots part with Mobility above zero, and Legs - the variant every insect, spider and
       crab is built from - is declared VariantOf="Feet" with Mobility="0". So a fire ant queen has
       six legs in its anatomy and cannot be knocked off any of them. The Mobility clause excludes as
       much as the missing-limb one does.

       Grounding is SILENT unless the target is airborne when it lands. Grounded.Apply prints nothing
       itself; the "falls to the ground" line comes from Flight.Fall, which only fires on a live
       Flying effect. Play-testing read as three failures in a row before examining a target showed
       the Grounded status sitting there the whole time. Anything written about this shell should say
       so, because a player will draw the same wrong conclusion. GroundOnHit needs ChargeUse="0" written out: it is an IPoweredPart
       whose constructor sets ChargeUse 100, and on a projectile with no power source IsReady fails
       and the part does nothing, silently. Vanilla's only user, ProjectileNaserCannon, writes the
       same zero for the same reason.

       Unlike the arrows these ARE craftable, and that is the same rule read the other way: tinkering
       is the artifact skill, and a cartridge serves a firearm, which is recovered technology. Vanilla
       agrees - Shotgun Shell and Lead Slug both have recipes while no arrow in the game does.

       Where the RECIPE sits is a separate decision from what it costs, and both are set here.
       TinkerItem.LoadBlueprint reads Tier from the BuildTier parameter, defaulting to the highest bit
       level in the cost, and DataDisk.GetRequiredSkill needs Tinker 2 past tier 3. Plain shot is one
       scrap electronics for five at tier 0 - the same slot as every grenade mk I - and a payload has
       no business being learnable there.

       All four cost 003: two scrap metal and one pure alloy, for three shells. That sits between
       vanilla's two anchors, a grenade mk I at two bits for one and plain shot at one bit for five,
       which is where a shell carrying an arrow's payload belongs. Bits alone would then put every
       shell at tier 3, with the Pump Shotgun, inside Tinker 1.

       That is right for the takedown shell and too early for the other three, so incendiary, cryo and
       flechette carry BuildTier="4" and sit with the Combat Shotgun behind Tinker 2 while still
       costing 003. The less-lethal round is the one anybody who can build the gun can also load;
       burning, freezing and defeating armour wait for the second skill.

       BuildTier is a real public field on TinkerItem that vanilla never writes, so it is worth saying
       why it is safe: it is read as a blueprint parameter by LoadBlueprint, and it is in the API
       snapshot the part-attributes check validates against. Its only effect is the recipe's tier, and
       decoupling that from the cost is the entire point here - the alternative, 004, would have
       charged pristine electronics for a paper cartridge.

       All four carry ExcludeFromTurretStock, and the case is stronger here than it was for arrows.
       Shotgun Shell is the ONLY blueprint in the game with AmmoShotgunShell and it carries no
       exclusive tag, so the shell pool is a single entry: untagged, these four would be four fifths
       of every shotgun turret's ammunition. TurretTinker also falls back to "Pump Shotgun" twice,
       which makes shotgun turrets the game's default. Note the scope, which is narrower than
       "turrets" - only TurretTinker and PlaceTurretGoal raise GenerateIntegratedHostInitialAmmo, so
       a turret you deploy yourself arrives empty and you load it from your own pack. -->

  <object Name="Raven_Incendiary Shell" Inherits="Projectile">
    <part
      Name="Render"
      DisplayName="{{blaze|incendiary}} shell"
      ColorString="&amp;R"
      DetailColor="w"
      Tile="items/sw_shotgun_shell.bmp"
